WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.720
FACEBOOK:
What are the first words that come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:08.480
mind when you think of Facebook as a whole? Maybe&nbsp;
powerful, ubiquitous, and privacy intrusion? Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:15.040
what are the first words that come to mind when&nbsp;
you think about OG Facebook platform? Old, dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.760
out of fashion maybe? Well, you’re not alone.&nbsp;
This sentiment seems to be pretty common online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.680
especially amongst younger generations. The&nbsp;
general theme seems to be that Facebook used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.680
to be something that was popular back in the&nbsp;
late 2000s and early 2010s, but since then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.160
it has largely fallen out of favor. And Google&nbsp;
trends simply confirms this idea. Interest in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:43.960
Facebook today is only a mere 16% of what it used&nbsp;
to be back in 2012. So, it’s a good thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:49.920
Facebook bought Instagram and WhatsApp and evolved&nbsp;
their business right? Well, there’s no arguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.560
that Instagram and WhatsApp weren’t stellar&nbsp;
acquisitions but what might surprise you is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:02.400
Facebook is still more lucrative than WhatsApp and&nbsp;
Instagram combined. In fact, OG Facebook is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.960
the most lucrative social media in the entire&nbsp;
world by a long shot. In 2023, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:15.280
Facebook is expected to pull in over $70 billion&nbsp;
in revenue. For perspective, YouTube only pulls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:23.160
in $30 billion, TikTok only pullin $13 billion,&nbsp;
and Twitter only pulls in $4.7 billion. The only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:30.600
platform that even comes close is Instagram at&nbsp;
$50 billion, but even Instagram is $20 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:36.560
behind Facebook. And that’s just looking at top&nbsp;
line numbers. If we look at revenue per user,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.680
Facebook becomes even more wild. It turns&nbsp;
out that Facebook still somehow has 3 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:49.840
monthly active users, the highest of social media&nbsp;
platform. As such, there revenue per user is $23&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.760
per year. For perspective, TikTok stands at about&nbsp;
$8 per user per year and even YouTube only stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:02.840
at $11 per user per year. So, who exactly are all&nbsp;
these people who are still using OG Facebook and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:14.308
how has Meta managed to keep Facebook so&nbsp;
profitable despite the overall sentiment?

00:02:14.308 --> 00:02:16.040
OLD IS GOLD:
One of the biggest secret weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:21.640
of Facebook is the time period in which it was&nbsp;
founded. At this point, it’s been nearly 20 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:27.320
since Facebook was founded which isn’t all that&nbsp;
long for a company but it’s basically eons for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:33.760
a social network. In fact, Facebook is by far the&nbsp;
oldest social media that’s still relevant to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:40.240
day. Everything else from that era whether its MSN&nbsp;
messenger or MySpace are completely dead. Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:45.680
actually started off as a Tinder sort of platform.&nbsp;
Originally, it was called FaceMash and it allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.560
Harvard Students to rate their peers in terms of&nbsp;
attractiveness. When you visit the website, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.640
would get two pictures side by side and you would&nbsp;
get to choose who was more attractive. Fun fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.400
Zuckerberg actually almost got expelled from&nbsp;
Harvard due to FaceMash as Harvard felt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:06.400
the website violated their student’s privacy and&nbsp;
security. Sounds like somethings never change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:13.280
But anyway, Facemash and later Facebook was a&nbsp;
young persons website. I mean, I can’t imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:18.720
any normal 40 or 50 year playing this game. But&nbsp;
here’s the thing, it’s not only Facebook that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:25.000
grown old over the years but all of it’s users as&nbsp;
well. If you were 20 to 30 when Facebook came out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:31.800
you’re now 40 to 50. And for the longest time,&nbsp;
this was actually seen a massive disadvantage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:37.520
To stay relavant, social networks are constantly&nbsp;
trying to get newer generations on board. YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:42.360
has managed to do a pretty good job of this given&nbsp;
that YouTube is commonly used by people of all age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:48.080
groups. TikTok on the other hand very much skews&nbsp;
towards younger demographics as you would expect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:54.680
In fact, nearly 70% of TikTok users are under&nbsp;
the age of 35. Facebook, however, largely skews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.680 --> 00:04:01.200
towards older demographics. Their most popular&nbsp;
demographics is age 25 to 34 and nearly half of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.880
all Facebook users are above the age of 35 which&nbsp;
is basically unheard of when it comes to social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:13.240
media. But, while it may not be cool to have a&nbsp;
platform filled with people over the age of 35,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:18.000
it’s turns out that it’s rather lucrative given&nbsp;
that this demographic is a lot more likely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.800
have money. In fact, for people under the age of&nbsp;
35, the median net worth is only $39,000. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:32.880
when you jump up to 35 to 44 bracket, this over&nbsp;
triples to $135,000 and that’s just the median.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:40.120
The average goes upto $550,000 and it only goes&nbsp;
up from there up until 75. And we see a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:45.040
story when we take look at the income of people&nbsp;
using these platforms. With YouTube for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:50.760
it’s actually less likely that you use YouTube&nbsp;
with growing income. 76% of people making less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:57.600
than $50,000 per year use YouTube, but only 69%&nbsp;
of people making $50 to $100,000 use YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:03.520
and 65% of people making over $100,000 use&nbsp;
YouTube. This isn’t to say that only broke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.080
people watch YouTube but rather that YouTube&nbsp;
tends to be watched more by students and young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.480
adults who don’t yet have substantial&nbsp;
income. With Facebook on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.800
there seems to be no correlation between income&nbsp;
and usage. In fact, whether you're making less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:24.960
than $30,000 per year or over $100,000 per year,&nbsp;
there’s about an 86% chance that you use Facebook.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.520
For obvious reasons, this is a much more valuable&nbsp;
audience to market to than teenagers and college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:36.000
students leading to far more ad revenue for&nbsp;
Facebook per user than any other platform.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:48.661
But all of that only tells half the story as&nbsp;
Facebook has an even more powerful secret.

00:05:48.661 --> 00:05:50.720
AN EVERYTHING APP:
Every tech companies’ dream is to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:57.320
an everything app like WeChat in China, but most&nbsp;
efforts to do so in the west simply end up falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:03.120
flat. It turns out that people simply don’t want&nbsp;
certain things to be connected and that separation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:09.640
is often not only useful but desired. In fact, one&nbsp;
of the main reasons that Instagram, SnapChat, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:16.000
eventually TikTok grew in popularity is because it&nbsp;
wasn’t connected to Facebook. Teenagers and young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:22.360
adults are naturally not drawn to a social media&nbsp;
platform that their parents also use. But while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:27.800
Facebook hasn’t been able to create an everything&nbsp;
app across generations or across industries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:32.280
it does seem like Facebook has actually been doing&nbsp;
pretty well in terms of creating an everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:37.840
social media app for their core demographic.&nbsp;
Personally, I’ve never used Facebook messaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:43.720
and I don’t know even a single person who&nbsp;
does. Everyone I know just uses iMessage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:49.400
Instagram DMs, or maybe even WhatSapp. But it&nbsp;
turns out that messaging friends and family is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:55.000
actually the number 1 reason that people use&nbsp;
Facebook. Amongst Facebook’s core demographic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:00.840
72% of users use Facebook for messaging&nbsp;
which is over 2 billion people by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:07.200
and that’s just messaging. 1.9 billion people use&nbsp;
it to share photos and videos similar to Instagram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:12.800
and YouTube. 1.7 billion people use it keep up&nbsp;
to date with news and current events similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:19.200
Twitter. And 1.6 billion people even use it to&nbsp;
watch memes and funny content similar to TikTok.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:25.360
If you didn’t know, it’s not just Instagram that&nbsp;
has Reels, Facebook does too. So, it seems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:31.480
Facebok has created this sort of niche super&nbsp;
app that’s a combination of Instagram, YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:37.080
Twitter, iMessage, and even TikTok that are all&nbsp;
super popular amongst the people who actually use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.920
Facebook. This not only gives Facebook a lot&nbsp;
of control but also a lot of versatility when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:47.760
it comes to ads. They’re not limited to any one&nbsp;
format like YouTube for example. Pretty much the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:52.600
only control you have when it comes to YouTube ads&nbsp;
is how long the ad is and whether its skippable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:58.640
or not, that’s pretty much it. Facebook on the&nbsp;
other hand is able to accommodate for image ads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:04.640
video ads, carousel ads, instant experiences, and&nbsp;
collections. Not to mention, they have several sub&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:09.880
formats under each of these larger formats. In&nbsp;
other words, on the advertiser side of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:15.400
Facebook is able to appeal to all sorts of&nbsp;
advertisers, and on the user side of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:20.760
they can push ads through all sorts of&nbsp;
formats. So basically, Facebook is super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:26.120
popular amongst an older richer demographic that&nbsp;
use Facebook for all of their social media needs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:31.120
and advertisers are able to reach these&nbsp;
people through any medium they want. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:44.421
you can start see just why Facebook is so darn&nbsp;
lucrative, but how sustainable really is this?

00:08:44.421 --> 00:08:46.320
A SOCIAL EVOLUTION:
Having a rich older demographic that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:53.040
loyal is great for the time being, but eventually,&nbsp;
these people will grow out of your platform right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:58.560
It’s no secret that as you grow older, you spend&nbsp;
less time on social media, the internet, and tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:04.280
in general right? Well, that has definitely&nbsp;
been the trend over the past few decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:10.280
but correlation does not indicate causation.&nbsp;
Older demographics not using social media is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:15.400
chaulked up to people simply growing out of it,&nbsp;
but as we can see with Facebook’s demographics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:21.080
people don’t necessarily grow out of social media.&nbsp;
As such, I suspect that older demograhpics not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:27.400
using social media actually has to do with other&nbsp;
aspects. First of all, people simply get busier as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:32.160
they grow older. They’re working harder than ever,&nbsp;
they have kids, they have to maintain their homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:37.560
and all of that. On average, people between&nbsp;
the ages of 35 and 44 have the least amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:43.240
of leisure time of any age group. But, after&nbsp;
this age, leisure time starts to grow once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:47.840
again. People get to a comfortable position in&nbsp;
their careers, their kids go off to college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:52.960
they pay off their homes, and so on. By the time&nbsp;
that they’re 65, their leisure time is higher than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:59.400
ever, and it seems that traditionally all of this&nbsp;
free time has just been filled with more TV. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:04.600
I think a lot of that simply has to do with&nbsp;
lifelong influences. When baby boomers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:09.880
the silent generation were growing up, TVs were&nbsp;
just starting become popular and that stuck with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:14.600
them. Similarly, as millennials and younger&nbsp;
gen Xers were growing up, social media was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:20.600
just started get popular, and that may very well&nbsp;
stick with them. I mean, we can already see this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:26.520
playing out. If you ask anyone under the age of&nbsp;
30 about Facebook, they’ll likely respond with:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:31.800
who even uses Facebook nowadays. In fact, that’s&nbsp;
probably why a lot of you clicked this video, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:38.240
assumed that Facebook is on its way out. But it&nbsp;
turns out that over 3 billion people use Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:44.640
on a monthly basis, much of whom are over the age&nbsp;
of 35. Of course, their use of the platform has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:50.200
evolved over time. They’re no longer ranking&nbsp;
whether their peers are hot or not. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:54.680
they’re in Facebook groups with their friends,&nbsp;
family, and neighbors, and they talk about their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.680 --> 00:11:00.520
kids growing up or having to replace to roof&nbsp;
or local events. And I don’t think this will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:06.000
change. In fact, as Facebook’s core demographic&nbsp;
grows older and starts having more leisure time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:11.000
they may actually start using Facebook even more&nbsp;
similar to how older generations started to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:16.040
TV more. In other words, Facebook could very&nbsp;
well be the first social media platform that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:21.560
used primarily by people in their 50s, 60s and&nbsp;
70s because for the first time, we’re seeing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:27.880
tech literate generation reach those ages. And&nbsp;
that’s not as far away as you might think. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:34.000
oldest millennials are about to turn 44 years old&nbsp;
meaning 50 is just around the corner. Social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.160
usage for people of the age of 65 for example&nbsp;
has already over quadrupled over the past decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:44.520
and I think that number will only grow as newer&nbsp;
generations reach those ages. Now of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:48.840
this doesn’t solve the problem of what&nbsp;
will happen to Facebook after millennials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:55.040
but what it does show is that Facebook may very&nbsp;
well stay dominant for a lot longer than anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:59.960
expected. And in the meantime, millennials and&nbsp;
younger Gen Xers should simply get richer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:05.160
richer and have more free time to spend up&nbsp;
until retirement atleast. So, while it may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:09.520
seem like Facebook is heading out the door,&nbsp;
it’s very possible that everything we’ve seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:16.320
so far is only half the story, and we’re about&nbsp;
to witness a social media evolution like never&nbsp;&nbsp;

